# Target C-II — shallow outer teeth force distinct internal penetration contacts

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Repository base:** `815c8b6ce3c4d8ce1b5c256f384e85811e25f995`  
**Status:** proof-only Type-II consequence; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** Type-II near-disk branch; numerical corollary also uses the current proof-only AG42/§171 low-`t` floor  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The preceding shallow-tooth theorem produces a linear family of inward unit contacts,
but its raw count loses a factor two when teeth are assigned to apexes and another
factor two when selected contacts are deduplicated.  Type-II near-disk geometry removes
both losses.

The key new observation is local and weight-free:

> a two-edge outer-tooth apex has no loaded unit-contact neighbor other than the two
> endpoints of its own base.

Thus every tooth apex has an internal unloaded-to-unloaded contact.  For sufficiently
shallow teeth that contact ends a fixed positive distance inside the local supporting
line, so its inner endpoint cannot itself be a shallow tooth apex.  Selecting one such
contact per shallow tooth is therefore injective.

The result upgrades the current low-`t` tooth alternative from

\[
0.0730476D-o(D)
\]

distinct penetration contacts to the explicit bound

\[
\boxed{0.3883231D-o(D)}
\]

distinct **internal** penetration contacts, with a simultaneous local-normal
penetration flux above `0.279D`.

---

## 1. Fixed-scale local flatness of the Type-II loaded hull

Canonical Type II gives centers `o_D` and numbers

\[
r_D=D/2-o(D),\qquad R_{+,D}=D/2+o(D)
\]

such that

\[
B(o_D,r_D)\subset F_D\subset B(o_D,R_{+,D}).
\tag{IP.1}
\]

The proof of the audited Section-124 chord lemma is uniform on every fixed Euclidean
scale.  We record the exact consequence needed below.

### Lemma IP.1 — fixed-scale loaded-boundary flatness

Fix `L<infinity`.  Let `a_D b_D` be a loaded-hull side with
`|a_D-b_D|<=2`, and let `c_D` be any loaded-hull vertex with
`|c_D-a_D|<=L`.  Then, uniformly over all such choices,

\[
\operatorname{dist}(c_D,\operatorname{line}(a_Db_D))=o(1).
\tag{IP.2}
\]

Moreover, if `c_Dd_D` is another loaded-hull side with both endpoints at bounded
distance from `a_D`, then

\[
\angle(\operatorname{line}(a_Db_D),
       \operatorname{line}(c_Dd_D))=o(1).
\tag{IP.3}
\]

### Proof

Translate `o_D` to the origin and put

\[
\beta_D=\arccos(r_D/R_{+,D})=o(1).
\]

For two loaded vertices `p,q` with `|p-q|<=L'`, their smaller radial-angle separation
is at most

\[
2\arcsin\frac{L'}{2r_D}=o(1)
\tag{IP.4}
\]

for every fixed `L'`.  Section 124 proves that every outward side normal at a boundary
point makes angle at most `beta_D` with the radial direction there.  Consequently all
oriented side directions on a fixed-scale short polar boundary window lie in an
angular interval of width `o(1)`, uniformly.

For the short boundary arc from `a_D` to `c_D`, projection onto the midpoint direction
of that interval gives

\[
\operatorname{length}(\text{arc}(a_D,c_D))
\le {L\over\cos(o(1))}=O_L(1).
\tag{IP.5}
\]

The side `a_Db_D` lies in the same fixed-scale tangent window.  Projecting the arc in
the normal direction to `a_Db_D`, its total transverse displacement is therefore

\[
O_L(1)\sin(o(1))=o(1),
\]

which proves (IP.2).  Applying the same tangent-window estimate to the two sides gives
(IP.3).  `square`

This is only the finite-scale form of the already-audited Section-124 geometry; no
quantitative rate for the Type-II Hausdorff convergence is required.

---

## 2. A tooth apex has exactly two loaded contact neighbors

Take any actual two-edge outer tooth

\[
a-x-b,
\]

where `a,b` are consecutive loaded-hull vertices, `x` is unloaded,

\[
|a-x|=|x-b|=1,
\qquad
s:=|a-b|\in(1,2].
\]

Audited outer-tooth exactness identifies this with one augmented triangular face.
Choose coordinates with its supporting line horizontal:

\[
a=(-u,0),\qquad b=(u,0),\qquad x=(0,h),
\]

where

\[
u=s/2\in(1/2,1],
\qquad
h=\sqrt{1-u^2}.
\tag{IP.6}
\]

### Lemma IP.2 — no third loaded neighbor

For all sufficiently large Type-II survivors, if a loaded vertex `c` satisfies
`|c-x|=1`, then

\[
c\in\{a,b\}.
\tag{IP.7}
\]

### Proof

Since `|a-x|=|x-c|=1`,

\[
|a-c|\le2.
\]

Lemma IP.1 with `L=2` gives, in the coordinates above,

\[
c=(X,z),\qquad z=o(1).
\tag{IP.8}
\]

The contact equation gives

\[
1=X^2+(z-h)^2,
\]

hence, using `u^2+h^2=1`,

\[
X^2=u^2+2hz-z^2=u^2+o(1).
\tag{IP.9}
\]

Because `u>=1/2`, this implies uniformly

\[
|X|=u+o(1).
\]

Thus `c` lies at distance `o(1)` from either `a` or `b`.  Source unit separation makes
that impossible for a distinct source point once `D` is large.  Therefore `c=a` or
`c=b`.  `square`

Canonical C26 gives every unloaded contact-core vertex degree at least three.  Hence
`x` has a contact neighbor `w` distinct from `a,b`.  Lemma IP.2 forces `w` to be
unloaded.  Therefore

\[
\boxed{xw\text{ is an internal contact edge of the unloaded component containing }x.}
\tag{IP.10}
\]

There is also no possibility that one apex supports two different outer teeth: a
second tooth would require another pair of loaded contact neighbors.  Consequently

\[
\boxed{\text{two-edge outer teeth have pairwise distinct unloaded apexes.}}
\tag{IP.11}
\]

For arbitrary teeth, selecting one internal edge per tooth already gives at least
`N_2/2` distinct internal edges, because one undirected edge has only two endpoints.
The shallow case below removes this final factor two as well.

---

## 3. A shallow tooth's internal contact reaches a deep endpoint

Fix

\[
0<\eta<\eta_0:=2(1-\cos(\pi/12))
=0.0681483474\ldots
\tag{IP.12}
\]

and call a tooth `eta`-shallow when

\[
s\ge2-\eta.
\]

Write

\[
\alpha=\arccos(s/2),
\qquad
0<\alpha\le\alpha_\eta:=\arccos(1-\eta/2)<\pi/12.
\tag{IP.13}
\]

Then the coordinates above become

\[
a=(-\cos\alpha,0),\quad
b=(\cos\alpha,0),\quad
x=(0,\sin\alpha).
\]

Take any extra contact neighbor `w` of `x`.  By Lemma IP.2 it is unloaded, and harmonic
trapping places it in `F`.  Write

\[
w-x=(X,Y),\qquad X^2+Y^2=1.
\]

The triangle-free inequalities `|w-a|>1` and `|w-b|>1` are exactly the ones used in
the preceding shallow-tooth theorem.  They give

\[
\boxed{
Y>\sin(\pi/3-\alpha)
\ge
Y_\eta:=\sin(\pi/3-\alpha_\eta).
}
\tag{IP.14}
\]

The previous theorem used only the edge component `Y`.  Here the absolute depth of the
inner endpoint is the stronger quantity

\[
\operatorname{depth}_{ab}(w)
=\sin\alpha+Y
>
\sin\alpha+\sin(\pi/3-\alpha).
\tag{IP.15}
\]

For `0<=alpha<pi/6`, the right side is increasing because

\[
\cos\alpha-\cos(\pi/3-\alpha)>0.
\]

Therefore every selected internal endpoint obeys the uniform bound

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{depth}_{ab}(w)>\sin(\pi/3)={\sqrt3\over2}.
}
\tag{IP.16}
\]

In contrast the shallow apex itself has depth

\[
\operatorname{depth}_{ab}(x)=\sin\alpha
\le\sin\alpha_\eta<\sin(\pi/12).
\tag{IP.17}
\]

Thus the selected contact crosses a fixed-width boundary layer.

---

## 4. The shallow selections are edge-injective

The only remaining duplication possibility is that an internal edge `xw` selected for
one shallow tooth is also selected for a shallow tooth whose apex is `w`.  Type-II
local flatness excludes this.

### Lemma IP.3 — the deep endpoint is not a shallow tooth apex

For every fixed `eta<eta_0` and all sufficiently large Type-II survivors, the endpoint
`w` in (IP.16) cannot be the apex of another `eta`-shallow outer tooth.

### Proof

Suppose `w` were the apex of a shallow tooth with loaded base `cd`.  Since

\[
|a-x|=|x-w|=|w-c|=|w-d|=1,
\]

both `c` and `d` lie within distance three of `a`.  Lemma IP.1 therefore gives

\[
\angle(ab,cd)=o(1)
\]

and, throughout this fixed-size neighborhood, the two supporting lines differ by
`o(1)` in normal position.  Because `|w-a|<=2`,

\[
\operatorname{depth}_{cd}(w)
=\operatorname{depth}_{ab}(w)+o(1)
>{\sqrt3\over2}-o(1).
\tag{IP.18}
\]

But a shallow tooth apex over `cd` has

\[
\operatorname{depth}_{cd}(w)
\le\sin\alpha_\eta
<\sin(\pi/12).
\tag{IP.19}
\]

The fixed gap between `sqrt(3)/2` and `sin(pi/12)` contradicts (IP.18)--(IP.19) for
large `D`.  `square`

Now choose one extra contact `xw` for every `eta`-shallow tooth.  The tooth apexes are
distinct by (IP.11).  If two choices gave the same undirected edge, the two tooth
apexes would have to be the two endpoints of that edge.  Lemma IP.3 forbids this.
Hence the choices are injective.

### Theorem IP.4 — internal penetration count

Let `N_2` be the number of two-edge outer teeth, `N_sh(eta)` the number of
`eta`-shallow teeth, `d_out=b-per(F)`, and `E_int(eta)` the number of distinct internal
contacts selected as above.  Then for every fixed `eta<eta_0` and all sufficiently
large Type-II survivors,

\[
\boxed{
E_{int}(\eta)
\ge N_{sh}(\eta)
\ge N_2-{d_{out}\over\eta}.
}
\tag{IP.20}
\]

Every selected edge has all three properties:

1. both endpoints are unloaded and lie in one positive-contact component;
2. its inward normal component relative to its tooth base exceeds `Y_eta`;
3. its inner endpoint lies more than `sqrt(3)/2` inside the tooth-base support line.

No KKT weight lower bound is used.

---

## 5. Current low-`t` numerical consequence

At the present proof-only floor

\[
t_{42}=C_{146}+0.0013751,
\]

the low-`t` squeeze gives

\[
{d_{out}\over D}\le0.002883975+o(1),
\tag{IP.21}
\]

while the tooth alternative of the exact tooth/long-side dichotomy gives

\[
{N_2\over D}
\ge0.4363893718701161\ldots-o(1).
\tag{IP.22}
\]

Choose the simple certified value

\[
\boxed{\eta=0.06<\eta_0.}
\]

Then (IP.20) gives

\[
\boxed{
{E_{int}(0.06)\over D}
\ge
0.4363893718701161\ldots
-{0.002883975\over0.06}
-o(1)
=0.3883231218701161\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{IP.23}
\]

For this tolerance,

\[
\alpha_\eta=\arccos(0.97)<\pi/12
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
Y_{0.06}
={\sqrt3\over2}(0.97)
-{1\over2}\sqrt{0.06-0.06^2/4}
=0.7184921838594732\ldots .
}
\tag{IP.24}
\]

Thus the sum of the selected edges' local inward-normal components is at least

\[
\boxed{
0.3883231218701161\ldots
\times0.7184921838594732\ldots D-o(D)
>
0.27900712D-o(D).
}
\tag{IP.25}
\]

The inner endpoints simultaneously lie more than

\[
0.8660254037844386\ldots
\]

inside their corresponding local hull support lines.

If only count is optimized, one may take any fixed `eta<eta_0` arbitrarily close to
`eta_0`; (IP.20) then approaches the stronger limiting density

\[
0.39407029724682\ldots .
\tag{IP.26}
\]

The explicit `eta=0.06` version is retained because it leaves comfortable angular
margin and nearly maximizes the simultaneous count-times-normal-component product.

---

## 6. Frontier effect

The tooth branch is now substantially less diffuse than the preceding theorem showed:

```text
low t + tooth alternative
 -> >0.38832 D shallow two-edge outer teeth
 -> >0.38832 D distinct unloaded shallow apexes
 -> >0.38832 D distinct internal unit contacts
 -> each crosses from a <0.244 boundary layer to depth >sqrt(3)/2
 -> aggregate local-normal penetration >0.27900 D.
```

The important change is that these edges belong to the **internal unloaded contact
graph**, not merely to the full contact graph.  They can therefore be inserted directly
into the canonical per-component quantities `e_r`, `delta_r`, `r_r`, and the pinned
stress identity

\[
s_r=e_r+t_r-2n_r-1.
\]

A closing theorem still has to convert this boundary-to-interior internal-edge family
into one of the currencies that global minimality cannot absorb.  The most direct next
target is a componentwise planar/diameter inequality that charges many such fixed-depth
penetration edges either to Eppstein slack `r_r`, to pinned surplus `s_r`, or to a
macroscopic attachment-cell diameter.  Unlike a weighted KKT sum, that target cannot
lose the new population through tiny contact weights.

The long-side alternative remains separate and open.

---

## 7. Reproducibility

Run

```bash
python3 research/verify_target_c_shallow_tooth_internal_penetration.py
```

for the displayed constants and angular margins.
